osed meld, not in hand. Half Flush: only one suit + honors. My exposed meld is B, hand is mostly T, so if I keep only T suit and B meld, that\u2019s two suits \u2014 invalid for Half Flush. So Half Flush impossible unless I change to all T + honors (discard B meld impossible).  \n- Discard terminal T9 to aim for All Simples.  \n\nGiven the All Chows possibility is close (need 1 more chow and pair), keep flexible:  \nKeep T3,T4,T5 (chow); T7,T8,T9 (chow); W2\u00d72 (pair candidate); W5,W6 (possible chow if draw W7).  \n\nBut W7 only 1 left. Better to aim for T chow: Keep T3,T4,T5 and T7,T8,T9\u00d72 (so T9 is extra for pair). Then need T4,T5,T6 chow or T5,T6,T7 chow \u2014 I have T5,T7, need T6; T6 has 4 left, good chance.  \n\nSo discard a W tile now to reduce suits. Which W tile?  \nW5 and W6 could combine with W7 for chow if drawn, but W7 rare. W2 pair is useful as pair. If I keep W2 pair, discard W5 or W6.  \n\nBut discarding W5 breaks possible W5-W6-W7 chow (low prob).  \n\nBetter: discard **W5** now because W6 can combine with W7,W8 (W7 1 left, W8 1 left) \u2014 slightly better? Actually W6 with W7,W8 needs both W7 and W8, low chance.  \n\nAlternatively discard T7? But T7 is part of T7-T9 chow.  \n\nActually maybe discard **W2** to reduce pairs and keep T9 pair \u2014 T9 is terminal, but All Simples gone anyway if I keep T9.  \n\nCheck tile safety: opponents\u2019 discards: F3, W9, etc. W2 not discarded much (prev player played W3, W1). W2 may be safe-ish.  \n\nBut to progress toward all chows: I need to keep T3,T4,T5 chow, T7,T8,T9 chow, T9 pair, and find T4,T5,T6 or T5,T6,T7. So I need T6.  \n\nHand after forming T3-T5 chow and T7-T9 chow and T9 pair:  \nT3,T4,T5 (chow1), T7,T8,T9 (chow2), T9 (pair), leftover: W2\u00d72, W5, W6.  \nThat\u2019s 3 melds (incl. B7-9 exposed) + pair = 4 melds? Wait:  \nExposed: B7-9 (meld1)  \nHand chow1: T3-T5 (meld2)  \nHand chow2: T7-T9 (meld3)  \nPair: T9 (meld4 is pair, but winning hand needs 4 melds + pair \u2014 so actually need 4th chow).  \nSo I\u2019m short 1 chow. So must get rid of W2\u00d72,W5,W6 by forming a chow from them or replacing them with T chow.  \n\nSo must draw T6 to make T5,T6,T7 chow (using T5,T7 in different sets?) \u2014 reorganize:  \nCurrently: T3,T4,T5 (chowA), T7,T8,T9 (chowB), T9 extra (pair). T5 used in chowA, so T5 can\u2019t be in new chow with T6,T7 unless break chowA. So maybe keep T4,T5,T6 chow: need T6, then T4,T5,T6 chow, T3 leftover to combine with? T3 could be pair with another T3 (none in hand), so maybe keep T3,T4,T5 chow as is.  \n\nThis is getting messy.  \n\nGiven time, safest: discard a less useful tile that doesn\u2019t break potential chows.  \n\n**W5** is isolated (only W5 in hand with W6, no W4, W7 rare). Discarding W5 reduces suit clutter.  \n\n**Reason:**  \nI have two ready chows in T suit and a pair (W2 or T9). Keeping T9 pair may block All Simples but is okay. W5 is least connected (W4 gone, W7 rare), so discard it to move toward needing only one more T chow (T4-T6 or T5-T7).  \n\n**Final choice:** Play W5.  \n\n---\n\n**Answer:** Play W5"}
